<section aria-label="Matrix multiplication sources and history">
<p>OpenAI released the <span set:html={math('2.258')}/> and <span set:html={math('9/4')}/> bounds in its October 6 collection. Their manuscripts are dated September 24 and October 2, respectively. The first-breakthrough mark uses the stronger bound from that shared release. Navot’s October 7 release extends <span set:html={math('9/4')}/> to every field. These dates record public releases, not manuscript dates or scientific priority.</p>
<details><summary>Earlier bounds and sources</summary>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02165411">Strassen (1969)</a> gives <span set:html={math('O(n^{\\log_2 7})')}/> scalar operations.</li>
<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.07970v1">Vassilevska Williams, Xu, Xu and Zhou (July 16, 2023)</a> give exponent <span set:html={math('2.371552')}/>.</li>
<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16349v1">Alman, Duan, Vassilevska Williams, Xu, Xu and Zhou (April 25, 2024; SODA 2025)</a> give exponent <span set:html={math('2.371339')}/>.</li>
<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16884">Dupont et al. with AlphaEvolve (August 17, 2026)</a> give exponent <span set:html={math('2.371177')}/>. This preprint supplies the classic bound above.</li>
</ul>
<p>The exponent bounds use the positive <span set:html={math('\\varepsilon')}/> slack in this page’s rule. The list shows selected earlier results, not a complete history.</p>
</details>
</section>
